# Numbers with SI units. A unit is written right after the number, in SI,
# with no space: `5kg`, `300cm`, `20°C`. A unit of more than one term is
# joined with `.` and takes `^` and a whole exponent, `kg.m^2.s^-2`, and
# there is no `/`. The number is any number a Scientific is, so
# `10.5(3)e-3kg` has its uncertainty, and it propagates through arithmetic
# as it does for a Scientific.
#
# A sum and a comparison convert the right side to the unit of the left; a
# product and a quotient combine the units; a result with no dimension is a
# plain number. Two quantities of different dimensions are an error.
#
# A prefix and a `.` on their own is a scale: `1k.` is 1000, `5m.` is 0.005,
# and the number it makes is exact. `m` alone is the metre and `m.` is milli.

sheet Literals

| Name         | Value        |
| ------------ | ------------ |
| mass         | 5kg          |
| length       | 300cm        |
| acceleration | 9.81m.s^-2   |
| energy       | 5kg.m^2.s^-2 |
| force        | 2kN          |
| temperature  | 20°C         |
| uncertain    | 10.5(3)e-3kg |
| kilo         | 1.5k.        |
| milli        | 5m.          |
| micro        | 10μF         |

sheet Arithmetic

| Name          | Value                     |
| ------------- | ------------------------- |
| sum           | = 5kg + 300g              |
| other way     | = 300g + 5kg              |
| difference    | = 5kg - 300g              |
| product       | = 2m * 3m                 |
| quotient      | = 10m / 4s                |
| scaled        | = 5kg * 2                 |
| power         | = 2m ** 3                 |
| root          | = sqrt(9m ** 2)           |
| no dimension  | = 6m / 3cm                |
| in grams      | = 5kg.to('g')             |
| in kelvin     | = 20°C.to('K')            |
| warmer by     | = 20°C - 10°C             |
| equal         | = 100cm == 1m             |
| ordered       | = 5kg < 6000g             |
| uncertain sum | = 3.0(3)m + 4.0(4)m      |
| plus-minus    | = 5kg ± 100g              |
| kilo of a sum | = 1.5k. + 500             |

# A column can be of a quantity type, here `Mass`: a `Quantity` of that
# dimension. A cell in another unit of it is converted to the column's unit, a
# bare number is read in that unit, and a cell of another dimension is an
# error. A column with no declared type, or `Quantity`, holds whatever it is
# given.
sheet Pantry

Item   : String
Weight : Mass

| Item  | Weight  |
| ----- | ------- |
| flour | 5kg     |
| sugar | 300g    |
| salt  | 5       |
| yeast | 7.5(2)g |

# The unit in brackets after the type is the unit the column is shown and held
# in, whichever the cells were written in: a bare number is read in it. After
# a `;` a number format as a Scientific takes may follow, `Mass[mg; .1f]`.
sheet Display

Item   : String
Kilos  : Mass
Grams  : Mass[g]
Metres : Length

| Item  | Kilos | Grams | Metres  |
| ----- | ----- | ----- | ------- |
| flour | 5kg   | 5kg   | 2m      |
| sugar | 300g  | 300g  | 150     |
| salt  | 5     | 5     | 3.5(2)m |

sheet Totals

| Name     | Value                      |
| -------- | -------------------------- |
| total    | = Pantry.Weight.sum()      |
| mean     | = Pantry.Weight.mean()     |
| largest  | = Pantry.Weight.max()      |
| smallest | = Pantry.Weight.min()      |
| in grams | = Pantry.Weight.sum().to('g') |

# A computed column of quantities may be declared too: it is held in the unit
# of its type, so the product of two lengths is an area in square metres
# whatever units the lengths were written in.
sheet Boxes

Width     : Length
Height    : Length
Area      : Area
Perimeter : Length

| Box   | Width | Height | Area | Perimeter |
| ----- | ----- | ------ | ---- | --------- |
| small | 20cm  | 30cm   | *    | *         |
| big   | 2m    | 150cm  | *    | *         |

Area      := Width * Height
Perimeter := 2 * (Width + Height)
